// SCOPE:plugin - scroll-to-latest hook for append-only feed regions.
package components

// AutoScrollExpr is the `data-init` expression the anchor runs. It is a
// const (not inlined into the templ) so the scroll rule has exactly one
// definition and a test can pin it: the assertion is on the string the
// browser compiles, not on a copy of it.
//
// An IIFE because the IIFE is the statement boundary. Datastar compiles the
// value as a function BODY (`Function(...names, expr)` — so a bare
// `if (…) { … }` happens to parse, as long as its braces balance), and it
// also rewrites a returnsValue expression as `return (<last statement>)`:
// wrapping the rule in one function keeps it a single statement whatever
// that rewrite does, and keeps the anchor's behavior readable as one unit.
// `el` is the element carrying the attribute (Datastar's own binding).
//
// prefers-reduced-motion is an explicit branch, not a note: smooth
// scrolling is a vestibular trigger, and a browser only suppresses it
// automatically for the CSS property, not for scrollIntoView's option.
const AutoScrollExpr = "(function () {" +
	"if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {" +
	"el.scrollIntoView();" +
	"} else {" +
	"el.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'end' });" +
	"}})()"

// AutoScroll renders the scroll-to-latest anchor for an append-only feed
// (the chat column).
//
// Why an element and not a listener: Datastar re-runs `data-init` on every
// node its element morph inserts. The anchor is the LAST child of the feed
// and travels inside the same patch that carries the new bubble, so the
// node that arrives with the newest message is the node that scrolls
// itself into view. That covers every patch shape the feeds use — append
// (chat's prompt echo, answer, error bubble) and outer replace (the Ask
// result region) — with zero vanilla JS, which is what keeps this out of
// the TestNoNewVanillaJS allowlist.
//
// Why no id: several anchors coexist (one per appended chat fragment), so
// an id here would duplicate on the page. The anchor is addressed only as
// `el` from its own expression, so it needs none.
//
// Why no `hidden` class: a `display:none` element has no box, and
// scrollIntoView on a boxless element is a no-op — the anchor must stay
// laid out. An empty div has no height and nothing to show, so it is
// already inert; only the assistive-tech opt-out is needed.
templ AutoScroll() {
	<div data-init={ AutoScrollExpr } aria-hidden="true"></div>
}
